      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great question! Training for a 5km is a bit different because the distance is shorter and the pace is, for most people, a little faster. Here\u2019s how you can adjust your plan:\n\n**1. Shorten your longest run:** Your weekly long run should be around 3\u20135 miles (5\u20138 km), rather than the longer distances needed for 10km training.\n\n**2. Add speed work:** Since 5ks are faster, doing intervals or tempo runs once a week helps with speed and stamina. For example, try 4x800m repeats at a pace faster than your target 5k pace with recovery in between.\n\n**3. Increase rest days or easy runs:** You can usually reduce your total weekly mileage and may even add an extra rest day to stay fresh.\n\n**4. Example week:**\n- Monday: Rest or cross training\n- Tuesday: Speed intervals or hill repeats\n- Wednesday: Easy run (2-3 miles)\n- Thursday: Rest or strength training\n- Friday: Tempo run (e.g., 2 miles at a challenging but controlled pace)\n- Saturday: Long run (4-5 miles)\n- Sunday: Optional easy run or rest\n\nFocus on quality (speed and intensity) rather than quantity. Good luck!",
          "user_rubrik": null,
