  {
    "runtag": "genaius-full-rewrite",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "4-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m curious about how to get ready before going for a run. Any basic steps?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I prepare to run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Before heading out for a run, there are a few basic steps you can follow to ensure a smooth and enjoyable experience, especially if you're not very confident in your fitness level. Start with a light warm-up to loosen your muscles\u2014this can include dynamic stretches or a brisk walk for 5-10 minutes. Hydrate adequately but avoid overdrinking right before running. Wear comfortable, well-fitted running shoes to prevent discomfort or injury. If you're new to running, consider alternating between walking and jogging to build endurance gradually. Lastly, set a realistic pace and focus on your breathing to stay relaxed. These steps will help you ease into running while building confidence in your fitness journey.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0019-16-15604:0": 15.162799835205078
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That helps! So, what would a typical week of workout look like if I wanted to prepare for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik": "What does a typical week of workout look like when preparing for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To prepare for a 10 km run, a typical week could include a mix of running, cross-training, and rest days. For beginners, aim for 4-5 days of training per week. Start with 2-3 running sessions, including one long run to build endurance. For example, you might do a shorter run (3-4 km) at a comfortable pace early in the week, a mid-distance run (5-6 km) midweek, and a longer run (7-8 km) on the weekend. Incorporate one day of cross-training, such as cycling or swimming, to improve overall fitness without overloading your legs. Include 1-2 rest or active recovery days, like light stretching or walking, to allow your body to recover. Gradually increase your mileage each week, but listen to your body and adjust as needed to avoid injury. This balanced approach will help you build confidence and endurance for your 10 km goal.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-68-19634:0": 21.809900283813477
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great details! Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "When choosing running shoes, comfort and fit should be your top priorities, especially if you're not very confident in your fitness level. A good pair of running shoes can prevent discomfort and injuries, making your runs more enjoyable. Experts recommend visiting a local running store to get personalized advice based on your foot type, gait, and running goals. Look for shoes that provide proper arch support and cushioning, and don't hesitate to test them out by jogging in-store. For beginners, neutral or cushioned shoes are often a great choice as they focus on comfort. Avoid focusing solely on brand or aesthetics; instead, prioritize functionality and how the shoes align with your needs. If you're disappointed with certain brands like Nike, exploring alternatives such as New Balance or HOKA might be worthwhile, as they are often praised for their comfort and durability. Investing in the right pair will help you build confidence and enjoy your running journey.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0035-42-12749:1": 22.497100830078125,
            "clueweb22-en0021-01-09812:1": 20.408199310302734,
            "clueweb22-en0017-01-00052:11": 19.0398006439209
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level.",
            "I often very disappointed with Nike product."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting, thanks! What's the difference between training for a 5km and a 10km run?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Training for a 5 km run focuses more on building speed and introducing running as a habit, while training for a 10 km run emphasizes endurance and gradually increasing mileage. For a 5 km, you might train 3-4 days a week, with shorter runs (2-4 km) and some speed intervals to improve pace. On the other hand, a 10 km plan typically involves 4-5 days of training, including one long run each week to build stamina, mid-distance runs, and possibly cross-training to avoid overuse injuries. The key difference lies in the weekly mileage and the focus on endurance for the 10 km, as it requires a longer sustained effort. Both plans should include rest days to allow recovery and prevent burnout.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0019-15-13298:0": 26.315000534057617
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you so much for your detailed and helpful answers! I really appreciate the information you\u2019ve shared. Best of luck with your running\u2014enjoy the journey, and take care! \ud83d\udc5f\ud83d\ude0a",
          "user_rubrik": null,
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": null,
