  {
    "runtag": "genaius-summary-rewrite",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "4-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I want to know how to get ready before going for a run. What should I do?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I prepare to run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To prepare for a run, start with a dynamic warm-up to get your body ready and reduce the risk of injury. Spend about 5-10 minutes doing light cardio, such as brisk walking or jogging in place, followed by dynamic stretches like leg swings, high knees, or arm circles. This helps increase blood flow and loosen up your muscles. Make sure you\u2019re wearing comfortable running shoes that provide proper support. Hydrate before heading out, but avoid drinking too much water right before running to prevent discomfort. If you\u2019re new to running or not confident in your fitness level, consider starting with a run-walk method, alternating between running and walking intervals. This approach can help you build endurance gradually and boost your confidence over time.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0035-72-08838:7": 19.456300735473633
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That sounds pretty approachable, especially the run-walk method. How often should people train in a typical week if they\u2019re aiming for a 10k race?",
          "user_rubrik": "What does a typical week of workout look like when preparing for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For a beginner aiming to complete a 10K race, training three to four times per week is a good starting point. Incorporating the run-walk method, as you mentioned, is an excellent approach to build endurance gradually while minimizing the risk of injury. A typical schedule might include two shorter runs during the week and one longer run on the weekend, with optional cross-training activities like swimming or cycling on other days. Rest days are equally important to allow your body to recover.\n\nIf you\u2019re following a run-walk plan, you can start with intervals like 1 minute of running followed by 1 minute of walking, gradually increasing the running time as your fitness improves. Over 8 to 10 weeks, you can build up to running longer distances comfortably. Remember, consistency is key, and listening to your body will help you avoid overtraining. Let me know if you\u2019d like help finding a specific training plan tailored to your needs!",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0005-57-00569:3": 25.96030044555664,
            "clueweb22-en0005-57-00569:4": 25.26259994506836,
            "clueweb22-en0005-57-00569:2": 24.588499069213867
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that's helpful. Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "When choosing running shoes, it\u2019s important to prioritize comfort, support, and durability. Since you\u2019re preparing for a 10K, look for shoes designed for long-distance running. Brands like Nike offer a variety of options, but given your past disappointment with their products, you might want to explore alternatives like New Balance or Brooks, which are known for their cushioning and stability. If you\u2019re unsure, visiting a specialty running store for a gait analysis can help you find the best fit for your needs. Let me know if you\u2019d like more specific recommendations!",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0016-83-09406:2": 23.327800750732422
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level.",
            "I often very disappointed with Nike product."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Definitely skipping Nike, they never work for me. Do you know any specific Brooks or New Balance models that are good for beginners?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "Skipping Nike is a great choice for you, given your past dissatisfaction. For beginner-friendly models, I recommend the Brooks Ghost 14 and the New Balance Fresh Foam 860v12. The Brooks Ghost 14 is known for its comfortable, cushioned fit and versatility, making it ideal for easy runs or longer distances. The New Balance Fresh Foam 860v12 offers excellent support for overpronators and a smooth ride, which is great for beginners seeking stability. Both models are highly rated for everyday training and should suit your 10K preparation well. You can find these at specialty running stores or online retailers.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-86-15272:1": 23.470800399780273
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I often very disappointed with Nike product."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s exactly the info I needed, thanks! What\u2019s the best way to break in new running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "You\u2019re welcome! Breaking in new running shoes is an important step to ensure comfort and prevent injuries. Start by wearing them for short walks or light activities to let your feet adjust to the fit. Gradually increase the duration of your runs in the new shoes, starting with shorter distances and low-intensity runs. This helps the shoes mold to your feet and reduces the risk of blisters or discomfort. \n\nAdditionally, wearing moisture-wicking socks can help prevent blisters during this process. If you notice any specific pressure points or discomfort, consider using insoles or consulting a running store for adjustments. Taking it slow and listening to your body will make the transition smoother and more enjoyable.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0018-48-09172:6": 16.108200073242188
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I often very disappointed with Nike product."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Super clear, thanks! What about adjusting my workout plan for a 5km run? I need tips, not generic advice. The last answer wasn\u2019t really what I was looking for.",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To adjust your workout plan for a 5km run, focus on these key elements:\n\n1. **Intervals**: Incorporate short, high-intensity intervals (e.g., 6 x 400m at a fast pace with 90 seconds of rest). This builds speed and endurance.\n\n2. **Tempo Runs**: Run at a comfortably hard pace for 15-20 minutes. This improves your lactate threshold, helping you sustain effort during the race.\n\n3. **Long Runs**: Include a weekly long run of 6-8km at an easy pace to build endurance.\n\n4. **Hill Repeats**: Add hill sprints early in your plan to build strength and improve running economy.\n\n5. **Rest and Recovery**: Take at least one rest day per week and reduce training volume every 3-4 weeks to avoid burnout.\n\n6. **Progression**: Gradually increase intensity and volume over 8-12 weeks, ensuring your body adapts safely.\n\nThese structured workouts will help you train smarter and achieve your 5km goal.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-38-13121:5": 20.903900146484375
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level.",
            "I often very disappointed with Nike product."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you for sharing your questions and sharing your preferences with me! I hope these tips help you prepare and enjoy your running journey\u2014whether it\u2019s 5K or 10K. If you ever need more targeted advice or have other fitness questions, feel free to reach out. Wishing you happy and successful running! Goodbye!",
          "user_rubrik": null,
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
