      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That didn\u2019t help answer my last question at all. Could you tell me more specifically how a weekly workout plan looks for someone preparing for a 10 km run? Like what days to run, rest, or cross-train?",
          "user_rubrik": "What does a typical week of workout look like when preparing for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For someone new to running or not very confident in their fitness, a common 10K training week looks like this: Monday (Rest), Tuesday (Short run, e.g., 1-3 miles), Wednesday (Cross-training, such as cycling or swimming), Thursday (Short run), Friday (Rest), Saturday (Short run), and Sunday (Long run, gradually increasing distance each week). Cross-training helps build endurance and prevent injury, while rest days are crucial for recovery. The long run on Sunday increases by about half a mile each week, peaking at 5-6 miles before the race. If you feel tired, you can swap a cross-training day for an extra rest day. This structure is designed to be manageable and build confidence for beginners [clueweb22-en0021-42-04337:1][clueweb22-en0021-42-04337:2][clueweb22-en0021-42-04337:3]. Do you want tips for cross-training activities?",
