        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019d love hydration tips, but could you also mention what to do if you have a sensitive stomach or food allergies before a run?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "For hydration before a 5km run, drink water steadily in the hours leading up to your run, and if you have a sensitive stomach, consider watered-down sports drinks or all-natural options. If you have food allergies or sensitivities, stick to bland, carb-based snacks and avoid high-fiber, high-fat, or unfamiliar foods before running. Are you interested in post-run recovery nutrition tips?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0031-24-03437:0": 0.03516372066067806,
            "clueweb22-en0045-76-10623:7": 0.03128054740957967,
            "clueweb22-en0029-83-10857:5": 0.03021353930031804,
            "clueweb22-en0005-12-13264:3": 0.0287391216352965,
            "clueweb22-en0015-76-09183:17": 0.027526395173453996
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am allergic to peanuts.",
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 9,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yes, post-run recovery tips would be great! Also, sometimes I get a really runny nose during and after running\u2014any idea why that happens or tips to deal with it?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "For optimal post-run recovery, aim to eat a snack or meal containing both carbohydrates and protein soon after your run\u2014this helps replenish glycogen stores, repair muscle micro-tears, and replace lost electrolytes. Good options include yogurt with fruit, smoothies, protein shakes, or fish like tuna or salmon (which you prefer over meat) [clueweb22-en0001-29-15914:1][clueweb22-en0012-09-10132:11]. Be sure to hydrate with water or an electrolyte drink, especially if you sweat a lot. If you have a sensitive stomach or food allergies, stick to simple, familiar foods and avoid anything that has caused issues in the past. As for your runny nose, this is known as exercise-induced rhinitis and is quite common, especially when running outdoors due to pollen or irritants. You can try running indoors on high-pollen days or consult a healthcare professional about using a nasal spray before your run [clueweb22-en0005-26-11868:2].",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0001-29-15914:2": 0.03252247488101534,
            "clueweb22-en0001-29-15914:1": 0.03200204813108039,
