  {
    "runtag": "grilllab-agentic-gpt4.1",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "4-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m looking to learn how to prepare for a run. What\u2019s the first thing I should do?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I prepare to run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Before your run, start with a warm-up that includes dynamic stretches like walking lunges, high knees, or butt kicks, followed by a brisk five-minute walk to gradually raise your heart rate and loosen your muscles [DOC-2][DOC-4]. This is especially important if you're not confident in your fitness level, as it helps prevent injuries and makes running feel easier. Also, make sure you've eaten a light snack with slow-releasing carbohydrates about two hours before your run and stay hydrated throughout the day [DOC-3]. Choose a flat, familiar route and don't worry about running the whole time\u2014alternating between walking and running is a great way to start [DOC-3][DOC-4]. How should I structure my first few running sessions?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0008-29-00346:6": 0.035199824330259116,
            "clueweb22-en0034-56-03500:10": 0.03076923076923077,
            "clueweb22-en0026-69-09197:0": 0.029030910609857977,
            "clueweb22-en0020-39-06283:11": 0.025678061306371368
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level.",
            "I am allergic to peanuts."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That helps! For structuring\u2014should I run every day, or is it better to have rest days between runs when starting out?",
          "user_rubrik": "What does a typical week of workout look like when preparing for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "When starting out with running, rest days are just as important as your training days. Experts recommend that beginners should schedule at least two rest days per week to give your body time to recover and adapt to the new activity, which helps prevent injuries and overtraining [DOC-1][DOC-2][DOC-4]. Rest and recovery are integral to any good training plan, and you shouldn't feel guilty about taking days off\u2014your muscles need this time to rebuild and get stronger [DOC-3]. As you get more comfortable, you can consider adding active rest days with light activities like walking or cross-training, but always listen to your body and prioritize recovery [DOC-4]. What are some good cross-training activities for rest days?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-30-18644:8": 0.05113100444170054,
            "clueweb22-en0033-30-18644:9": 0.04458874458874459,
            "clueweb22-en0005-48-11149:2": 0.042285619688776074,
            "clueweb22-en0014-83-02028:17": 0.03762360135310955
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s useful, thanks! Actually, can you tell me what a typical week of workouts would look like if I\u2019m preparing for a 10 km run? Something practical, please.",
          "user_rubrik": "What does a typical week of workout look like when preparing for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For someone new to running and building confidence, a typical week in a beginner 10K plan might look like this: Monday\u2014Rest, Tuesday\u2014Short run (1-2 miles), Wednesday\u2014Cross-training (like cycling, swimming, or brisk walking), Thursday\u2014Short run (1.5-2 miles), Friday\u2014Rest, Saturday\u2014Short run (2-2.5 miles), Sunday\u2014Long run (gradually increasing, starting around 2-3 miles) [clueweb22-en0021-42-04337:1][clueweb22-en0032-17-06111:5]. This structure gives your body time to recover, helps prevent injury, and keeps the training manageable. Cross-training and rest days are key for beginners, and the long run on Sunday should slowly increase each week, never jumping by more than half a mile [clueweb22-en0021-42-04337:1]. What are some good cross-training activities for runners?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0021-42-04337:1": 0.07890107388058208,
            "clueweb22-en0021-42-04337:0": 0.06894371493128765,
            "clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:10": 0.06739652158521628,
            "clueweb22-en0032-17-06111:5": 0.0639929124583856
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yeah, thanks, got it. Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "The best running shoes for beginners are comfortable, supportive, and suited to your running style and foot shape. Visit a specialty running shop for a personalized fit, and look for shoes with good cushioning and stability. How often should I replace my running shoes?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0037-17-17377:3": 0.03681114551083591,
            "clueweb22-en0026-47-01829:14": 0.03642993642993643,
            "clueweb22-en0017-51-11925:2": 0.033961319372540086,
            "clueweb22-en0040-46-18574:1": 0.03252247488101534
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful. Can you say more about brands? I\u2019m in Spain, so mention some that are easy to find here. And maybe avoid Nike, please\u2014they always disappoint me.",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "If you're looking for running shoes in Spain and want to avoid Nike, consider brands like Asics, Adidas, Brooks, and Saucony. Asics is highly regarded for its support and shock absorption, making it a favorite among long-distance and beginner runners. Adidas shoes are also popular in Spain and tend to run true to size or slightly bigger, which can be helpful for comfort. Brooks and Saucony are both known for their cushioning and stability, and their shoes are often recommended for new runners. All these brands are easy to find in Spanish sports stores and online retailers, offering a range of models suitable for different foot types and running needs [clueweb22-en0022-07-05016:1][clueweb22-en0009-83-14525:8]. Are you interested in specific beginner-friendly models from these brands?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0024-48-07675:10": 0.05238666351569578,
            "clueweb22-en0009-83-14525:8": 0.04649372261543647,
            "clueweb22-en0022-07-05016:1": 0.04323800318726016,
            "clueweb22-en0022-67-14763:9": 0.04249546826786478
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I live in Spain.",
            "I often very disappointed with Nike product.",
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yes, please! Models good for someone not very fit, and maybe something durable? Also, how do I know the right size?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "For new runners seeking comfort and durability, the Asics GEL-Cumulus, Adidas Ultraboost, Brooks Ghost, and Saucony Ride are all excellent choices and widely available in Spain. The Saucony Ride and Brooks Ghost are especially praised for their cushioning and support, making them ideal for those building fitness and looking for a shoe that will last through regular training [clueweb22-en0040-46-18574:1][clueweb22-en0017-51-11925:2][clueweb22-en0011-18-03914:9]. When choosing your size, it's important to have about a thumb\u2019s width of space between your longest toe and the end of the shoe, as feet can swell during running. Trying shoes on in the afternoon or evening, when your feet are at their largest, can help ensure a comfortable fit. If possible, visit a specialty running store for a personalized fitting, or use a 3D scan or wear analysis if available [clueweb22-en0004-94-13132:12].",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0040-46-18574:1": 0.04409057148368384,
            "clueweb22-en0017-51-11925:2": 0.03518075037062379,
            "clueweb22-en0035-42-12749:2": 0.031024531024531024,
            "clueweb22-en0011-18-03914:9": 0.03055037313432836,
            "clueweb22-en0004-94-13132:12": 0.02761904761904762
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level.",
            "I live in Spain.",
            "I often very disappointed with Nike product."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Perfect, I appreciate the details! What about clothing? What kind of gear helps if I tend to get a runny nose when running?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "If you tend to get a runny nose while running, opt for lightweight, breathable, and moisture-wicking clothing to stay comfortable and dry. Avoid clothing with latex fibers, as these can sometimes trigger contact allergies and worsen nasal symptoms [clueweb22-en0016-68-14805:2]. Carrying soft tissues or using a sweatband on your wrist can help you manage a runny nose on the go. If your symptoms are related to allergies, wearing a buff or lightweight scarf over your nose and mouth can help filter out pollen and other irritants, especially during high-pollen days [clueweb22-en0032-79-06723:0]. Running after 10 a.m. or after rain, when pollen counts are lower, can also help reduce symptoms. For persistent issues, consult a healthcare professional about using a nasal spray or antihistamine before your run [clueweb22-en0005-26-11868:2][clueweb22-en0016-68-14805:2].",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0016-68-14805:1": 0.05382391338675492,
            "clueweb22-en0032-79-06723:0": 0.045207704646168065,
            "clueweb22-en0016-68-14805:2": 0.031754032258064516,
            "clueweb22-en0005-26-11868:2": 0.027236652236652236
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I often have a runny nose in summer.",
            "I am not very confident in my fitness level.",
            "I live in Spain."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Nice, that\u2019s some new stuff for me! Is there any advice about what to eat the day before a run?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "To fuel your run, eat a dinner that includes complex carbohydrates (such as brown rice, whole grain pasta, or potatoes), lean protein (fish is a great choice for you), and cooked, easy-to-digest vegetables. Avoid fatty meats, heavy dairy, beans, and cruciferous vegetables, as these can cause stomach discomfort. Sample meals include fish with brown rice or salmon-stuffed baked potatoes. Don't overeat, and make sure your meal is familiar to you to avoid surprises on run day. The goal is to provide your body with energy without causing digestive issues the next morning. If you run in the morning, a light, carb-rich snack (like a banana or toast) 30-60 minutes before running can also help [clueweb22-en0021-50-04929:3][clueweb22-en0036-15-14902:4][clueweb22-en0044-23-06451:0][clueweb22-en0014-46-02248:10].",
          "citations": {
