      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That sounds helpful! What would a typical weekly running schedule look like?",
          "user_rubrik": "What does a typical week of workout look like when preparing for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "A typical weekly running schedule for someone not confident in their fitness should focus on gradual progression, injury prevention, and flexibility. Here\u2019s a beginner-friendly structure tailored to your needs:\n\n**Weekly Structure for Beginners**\n- **3\u20134 Runs per Week:** Start with 3 runs, using a run/walk method (e.g., run 30\u201360 seconds, walk 1\u20132 minutes; repeat for 20\u201330 minutes). This eases your body in and reduces injury risk ([Fleet Feet], [Run For Good]).\n- **1 Long Run:** Once per week, make one run slightly longer (but still comfortable). This builds endurance.\n- **Rest Days:** At least 2 rest days per week. Recovery is crucial, especially when starting out.\n- **Optional Cross-Training:** 1\u20132 days of low-impact activity (yoga, cycling, swimming) to boost fitness and reduce injury risk ([Fleet Feet], [Run For Good]).\n\n**Sample Week**\n- Monday: Rest or gentle cross-training\n- Tuesday: Run/walk (20\u201330 min)",
