  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5-bm25-debertav3-gpt5",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "4-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m looking for tips on how to get ready before going for a run. Any advice?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I prepare to run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "great to hear you \u2019 re getting into running. quick, beginner - friendly prep : - timing and weather ( spain ) : go early or late to avoid heat. spf 30 +, hat, sunglasses. - shoes / gear : comfortable, well - fitted running shoes and moisture \u2011 wicking socks. bring a phone / id ; reflective gear in low light. - warm - up ( 5 \u2013 10 min ) : brisk walk, then dynamic moves \u2014 leg swings, hip circles, ankle rolls, high knees. - start easy : try run \u2013 walk intervals ( e. g., 1 min run / 1 \u2013 2 min walk ) for 20 \u2013 30 minutes at a conversational pace. increase total time or run segments gradually ( about 10 % weekly ). rest days matter. - hydration : drink water beforehand ; small sips during. for runs > 45 minutes, add electrolytes. - pre - run snack ( peanut - free ) : 30 \u2013 90 min before, choose easy carbs \u2014 banana, yogurt, toast with jam, oatmeal, or rice cakes with sunflower seed butter. always check labels for cross \u2011 contamination, and avoid trying new foods right before a run. carry your epine",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0011-71-07456:2": -0.7803522348403931,
            "clueweb22-en0014-73-13507:1": -1.1527496576309204,
            "clueweb22-en0015-07-07781:0": -1.2727978229522705,
            "clueweb22-en0017-46-17522:1": -1.4861488342285156,
            "clueweb22-en0022-11-19182:0": -1.489823579788208
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that's helpful! What does a typical week of workout look like if I want to prepare for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik": "What does a typical week of workout look like when preparing for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "here \u2019 s a simple beginner - friendly weekly template you can repeat and gradually build toward a 10k : - monday : rest or gentle mobility - tuesday : easy run 4 \u2013 5 km ( 25 \u2013 35 min ). optional 15 \u2013 30 min lower - body strength ( squats, lunges, bridges, deadlifts ). - wednesday : cross - train 30 \u2013 40 min ( bike, swim, brisk walk, yoga ). - thursday : easy run 3 km ( 20 \u2013 25 min ). optional 15 \u2013 30 min upper - body / core. - friday : rest - saturday : cross - train 40 \u2013 60 min - sunday : long run at conversational pace, start 5 \u2013 6 km and add ~ 1 km each week up to 8 \u2013 9 km. use run \u2013 walk if needed. notes : - warm up 5 \u2013 10 min ( brisk walk + dynamic drills ) and cool down 5 \u2013 10 min every session. - keep weekly increases modest ( about 10 % or less ). every 3 \u2013 4 weeks, cut volume by ~ 20 \u2013 30 % for recovery. - race week : two short easy runs ( 3 km and 2 km ), one light cross - train day, then rest 1 \u2013 2 days before",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0005-48-11149:9": -1.0197842121124268,
            "clueweb22-en0019-21-12893:7": -1.4664424657821655,
            "clueweb22-en0019-21-12893:8": -2.3962674140930176,
            "clueweb22-en0014-85-03678:0": -3.2422351837158203,
            "clueweb22-en0036-01-02149:1": -3.2635703086853027
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Cool, thanks! Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "here \u2019 s a quick guide to choosing running shoes : - surface and weather : road vs trail. for wet / off - road, trail shoes with waterproof ( e. g., gtx ) uppers and a debris \u2011 blocking collar help ; for dry roads, choose breathable mesh. note : highly cushioned road shoes can stain and aren \u2019 t great off \u2011 road. - fit first : aim for a thumb \u2019 s width in front of your longest toe, secure heel, and enough width for your toes to splay ( a wider toe box improves comfort and weight distribution ). try shoes late in the day when feet are slightly swollen. - cushioning and drop : more cushioning = softer feel ; less = more ground feel. a moderate heel \u2011 to \u2011 toe drop ( about 8 \u2013 10 mm ) is beginner \u2011 friendly ; lower drops load calves more. - weight and stability : lightweight is nice, but prioritize stability through the heel and midfoot and flexible forefoot for smooth transitions. - breathability : in spain \u2019 s heat, choose airy mesh ; waterproof models are better for rain but run warmer. - insoles : removable insoles let you use orthotics. - replacement",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0038-31-10646:1": -1.0975546836853027,
            "clueweb22-en0012-85-18771:4": -1.4098336696624756,
            "clueweb22-en0012-85-18771:3": -2.0233561992645264,
            "clueweb22-en0028-89-20091:10": -2.0893406867980957,
            "clueweb22-en0028-89-20091:9": -2.287196397781372
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s great detail! Can a warm-up routine be too long or too short?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "short answer : yes. too short and you \u2019 ll feel tight and breathless ; too long and you \u2019 ll start the workout already fatigued. use these ranges : - easy runs : 5 \u2013 10 minutes brisk walk / jog + dynamic drills ( leg swings, hip circles, ankle rolls ). - long runs : 10 \u2013 15 minutes progressive jog + a few drills. - speed / tempo / hills / race : 15 \u2013 25 minutes easy jog + mobility + 4 \u2013 6 short strides ( 10 \u2013 20 seconds ) and 1 \u2013 3 brief pick - ups at session pace. weather tweaks : - cold : add 5 \u2013 10 minutes. - hot ( spain ) : keep it shorter / easier, sip water, and let the first kilometer finish the warm - up. good signs your warm - up is right : - breathing easy, light sweat, joints feel loose, first reps / kilometers feel smooth. too short if : - first 1 \u2013 2 km feel creaky, breathless, or niggles pop up. too long if : - legs feel flat before the session, hr is high, you \u2019 re already sweating hard or thirsty. rules of thumb : - warm - up ~ 10 % of easy - run duration",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0038-12-02534:2": -0.5473175048828125,
            "clueweb22-en0005-48-11149:9": -1.2126351594924927,
            "clueweb22-en0019-21-12893:7": -1.442307949066162,
            "clueweb22-en0038-12-02534:3": -1.445733904838562,
            "clueweb22-en0019-21-12893:8": -2.238574743270874
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Okay, so how do I adjust my workout plan specifically for a 5km run instead of a 10km run?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "great question. to pivot from 10k prep to 5k, lower volume a bit, keep a shorter long run, and add one focused 5k - pace workout weekly. weekly structure ( repeatable ) : - monday : rest or mobility - tuesday : 5k - specific workout ( see 6 - week progression below ) - wednesday : cross - train 30 \u2013 40 min ( bike, swim, brisk walk ) - thursday : easy run 20 \u2013 30 min + 4 \u2013 6 strides - friday : rest - saturday : cross - train 30 \u2013 45 min or easy jog 20 \u2013 25 min - sunday : long run easy 45 \u2013 60 min ( about 6 \u2013 9 km ), conversational pace 6 - week tuesday progression ( always warm up 10 \u2013 15 min, cool down 10 min ) : - week 1 : 5 x 400 m at 5k goal pace, 100 m easy jog between - week 2 : 4 x 600 m at 5k pace, 100 m jog - week 3 : 3 x 800 m at 5k pace, 200 m jog - week 4 : 6 x 400 m at 5k pace, 100 m jog ; last rep faster - week 5 : 4 x 800 m at 5k pace, 200 m",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0006-71-03556:9": -0.7997064590454102,
            "clueweb22-en0038-12-02534:2": -0.8143796920776367,
            "clueweb22-en0019-21-12893:7": -1.3569493293762207,
            "clueweb22-en0038-12-02534:3": -1.436185359954834,
            "clueweb22-en0004-71-10660:9": -1.4373043775558472
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
