          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I want to know how I should get ready before going for a run. Any tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I prepare to run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Good plan \u2014 cover gear, warm-up, hydration, pacing and safety.  Night-before prep - Lay out clothes, shoes, socks, reflective gear (if dark), charged watch/phone and any headgear.  Warm-up (always) - Do 5\u201315 minutes of dynamic warm-up: jumping jacks, high knees, leg swings, lunges and light jogging. Dynamic work readies muscles and reduces injury risk. Save long static stretches for after the run.  Hydration & fueling - Start hydrated and carry water or an electrolyte drink. A rough guideline is ~600\u2013700 ml/hour of sweat loss; adjust by temperature and effort. Bring a small snack for longer runs.  Pacing & progression - Start slow, use a run\u2011walk strategy if you\u2019re new, and increase running time gradually. Allow rest days and adapt the plan to your fitness.  Safety & comfort - Wear proper running shoes, bright/reflective clothing, tell someone your route, vary routes to avoid boredom, and carry ID/phone.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0019-16-15604:2": 28.86280059814453,
            "clueweb22-en0041-39-08184:5": 27.61389923095703,
            "clueweb22-en0008-29-00346:3": 26.461700439453125,
            "clueweb22-en0025-70-15571:0": 25.927000045776367,
            "clueweb22-en0024-39-15955:1": 25.454999923706055
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. Is there a weekly schedule you\u2019d recommend for getting ready for a 10k? Like how often should I run or rest?",
          "user_rubrik": "What does a typical week of workout look like when preparing for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes. Keep it simple and match it to your level.  Minimum (beginner, 3\u20134 days/week) - 2 easy runs (20\u201340 min) - 1 quality workout (tempo/intervals or hill repeats) - 1 long run (start ~30\u201340 min, build gradually) - 2 rest/cross\u2011train days + optional short recovery run  Intermediate (5 days/week) - Mon: Rest or cross\u2011train - Tue: Easy run + lower\u2011body strength - Wed: Speedwork (intervals, tempo, or lactate\u2011threshold work) - Thu: Rest or easy + upper\u2011body/core strength - Fri: Easy run (recovery pace) - Sat: Long run (build toward target distance/time) - Sun: Short recovery run or rest  Notes - Do one focused speed/threshold session weekly when targeting a faster 10K. - Progress long run and intensity gradually; introduce race\u2011specific workouts 4\u20136 weeks out. - Always include a 5\u201315 min dynamic warm\u2011up and at least one full rest day.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0014-61-01516:2": 63.43510055541992,
            "clueweb22-en0026-15-16607:4": 60.767601013183594,
            "clueweb22-en0038-65-08740:2": 59.395999908447266,
            "clueweb22-en0000-52-05962:7": 59.34130096435547,
            "clueweb22-en0019-21-12893:7": 58.80160140991211
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks! Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Good question \u2014 pick shoes based on your arch/pronation and gait.  - Figure your pronation/arch first: high arches/underpronation (supinators) need lots of cushioning; neutral arches can use many styles; low arches/overpronation need stability or structured-cushioning shoes.   - Shoe types to consider: cushioned/neutral models (curved or semi-curved last) for neutral or supinated feet; stability/motion-control models for overpronators.   - Example choices (brand-specific): neutral cushioned lines for comfort and flow; stability lines with dual-density support for overpronation; severe pronators may need maximal midsole support.   - Insoles: removable orthotic or supportive insoles can help flat feet, plantar fasciitis, shin splints or high-arch needs; gel pads and moisture-wicking liners add comfort.   - Fit tips: try shoes on with the socks you run in, test for a thumb\u2019s-width toe box, and if possible get a gait analysis in-store (or video) to confirm the best model.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0024-53-00659:0": 68.15570068359375,
            "clueweb22-en0029-24-00838:4": 67.64910125732422,
            "clueweb22-en0000-41-18655:3": 66.10299682617188,
            "clueweb22-en0016-64-12518:5": 66.08580017089844,
            "clueweb22-en0041-08-14281:0": 65.65730285644531
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019ve always found Nike running shoes a bit disappointing\u2014other brands have been comfier for me. Do you recommend any good alternatives that are popular in Spain?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "Totally reasonable \u2014 lots of runners prefer non\u2011Nike brands. Popular, well-regarded alternatives you\u2019ll find easily in Spain:  - Asics \u2014 strong cushioning and stability options; their performance models are top-rated for economy and long runs.   - Brooks \u2014 very comfy everyday trainers (Brooks Ghost is a common favorite).   - Adidas \u2014 widely available in Spain; Boost\u2011cushioned shoes are plush and responsive.   - New Balance \u2014 reliable, roomy fits and good variety for different foot shapes.   - Mizuno \u2014 solid build and a distinctive ride (works well for many runners).   - Saucony \u2014 comfortable road shoes (some people find models like the Mirage very suitable).   - Inov\u20118 / Salomon \u2014 if you do trails, these are popular off\u2011road choices.  Quick tips when switching brands: - Get a gait/fit check in a running shop (many use treadmill video).   - Try shoes with the socks you run in; allow a thumb\u2019s width in the toe box.   - Consider removable insoles (Ortholite/orthotic) if you need extra support or cushioning.   - Test with a short run or treadmill session before committing.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0031-60-13190:11": 30.506399154663086,
            "clueweb22-en0009-92-18444:0": 29.21109962463379,
            "clueweb22-en0009-92-18444:15": 28.024599075317383,
            "clueweb22-en0034-45-01558:0": 27.98040008544922,
            "clueweb22-en0009-92-18444:16": 27.760499954223633
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, thank you! What about post-run recovery\u2014what should I do right after finishing my workout?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Nice \u2014 good to plan this. Right after a run do these priority steps:  1. Cool down & move: walk 5\u201310 minutes to keep blood flowing and reduce tightness.   2. Rehydrate: drink water with electrolytes (especially after long or hot runs).   3. Refuel within ~30 minutes: a mix of carbs + protein (examples: recovery shake, yogurt + fruit, medjool dates and nut butter, applesauce + a protein snack, gels if needed).   4. Clean/change: get out of sweaty clothes to lower chill and skin issues.   5. Mobility & foam rolling: gentle rolling and light dynamic movements now; save long static stretches for after muscles have cooled a bit.   6. Recovery tools as needed: ice bath or compression for heavy efforts; sleep and quality rest are crucial in the hours/days after.   7. Plan recovery: easy activity or rest the rest of the day, and prioritize sleep and nutrition to repair and replenish.  Listen to your body \u2014 scale recovery based on effort, distance and how sore you feel.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0046-54-13461:5": 45.722599029541016,
            "clueweb22-en0046-54-13461:6": 42.495201110839844,
            "clueweb22-en0018-08-05431:9": 42.198299407958984,
            "clueweb22-en0022-46-13252:12": 41.824798583984375,
            "clueweb22-en0041-40-18666:4": 40.87889862060547
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great, I like that you mention sleep\u2014I always need a nap after running, especially midday. Any advice on helping with runny nose issues while exercising? I get this a lot in the summer.",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "that \u2019 s common \u2014 \u201c exercise \u2011 induced rhinitis \u201d often comes from nasal blood \u2011 vessel dilation and / or airborne irritants ( pollen, dust, heat, humidity ). practical things to try : - train indoors on high \u2011 pollen or very hot days ( or run early / late when pollen is lower ). - rinse your nose with saline before / after runs to wash out pollen / irritants. - try a nasal spray before workouts : a steroid spray for ongoing allergic rhinitis ( takes days / weeks to help ) or a short \u2011 term topical decongestant for occasional use ( avoid > 3 days ). discuss options with your doctor. - an oral non \u2011 sedating antihistamine can help if allergies are the cause. - wear a lightweight buff or mask in dusty / pollen conditions. - cool down and breathe through your nose where possible ; switching to indoor treadmill sessions when symptoms are worst can make a big difference. - if symptoms are severe, bloody, or limit training, see an ent or allergist for testing and tailored treatment ( immunotherapy, prescription sprays ). if you want, tell me where you run and sympt",
          "citations": {
