  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "4-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I want to know what\u2019s the best way to get ready for a run. Any advice?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I prepare to run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "great \u2014 here \u2019 s a simple, confidence - building plan to get ready for runs : - start small : 3 sessions / week. use walk - run intervals ( e. g., 1 \u2013 2 min run, 2 \u2013 3 min walk ) and gradually increase running time. - add one longer easy run per week and one progression run ( start slow, finish faster ) to build endurance and race - finish strength. - strength + mobility 2\u00d7 / week : bodyweight squats, lunges, planks, and dynamic warm - ups for hips / ankles. - warm up before each run ( 5 \u2013 10 minutes brisk walk + leg swings ), and cool down with light stretching afterward. - nutrition / hydration : hydrate well the day before, eat a light snack 1 \u2013 2 hours before ( avoid peanuts or peanut - containing products ), and don \u2019 t try new gels / foods on running day. - recovery : sleep, rest days, and watch pain \u2014 stop if something hurts and don \u2019 t overtrain. - practical tips : lay out gear the night before, choose comfortable shoes, keep a simple training log to track progress and build confidence. if you want, i can create a 4 - week beginner plan",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0012-94-13399:5": -3.3752665519714355,
            "clueweb22-en0039-01-13057:1": -3.509557008743286,
            "clueweb22-en0039-01-13057:2": -3.9200587272644043,
            "clueweb22-en0032-17-06111:15": -5.135715484619141,
            "clueweb22-en0012-12-14798:15": -5.418177127838135
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s really helpful, thanks! How many rest days would you usually take in a week with this kind of plan?",
          "user_rubrik": "What does a typical week of workout look like when preparing for a 10 km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Great question \u2014 for a 3-run beginner plan I recommend 1\u20132 full rest days each week plus one optional active-recovery day.   Practical guide: - Aim for 2 full rest days if you\u2019re not confident or feel extra tired (safe choice).   - If you feel okay, 1 full rest day + 1 active-recovery day (easy walk, gentle cycling, or mobility/foam-rolling) works well.   - Always keep at least one day free before or after your long run.  Example week: - Mon: Rest - Tue: Short run + strength - Wed: Mobility/strength (active recovery) - Thu: Easy run - Fri: Rest - Sat: Optional light cross-train - Sun: Long run  Listen to your body: increase rest if you\u2019re sore, unusually tired, or getting niggles \u2014 don\u2019t train through pain. Also prioritize sleep (your lunchtime nap can help recovery) and hydrate well; avoid peanut-containing snacks because of your allergy.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0005-48-11149:9": -1.1995551586151123,
            "clueweb22-en0025-99-19585:3": -1.9056618213653564,
            "clueweb22-en0016-58-13885:1": -3.9627773761749268,
            "clueweb22-en0025-99-19585:2": -4.199760437011719,
            "clueweb22-en0015-85-04910:2": -4.646768093109131
