          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Nice, thanks for the details. Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some information about running shoes?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "good \u2014 here \u2019 s a compact, practical guide to running shoes so you can choose what fits you and your goals. what to check when shopping - fit : heel secure, snug through the forefoot but with roomy toe box so your toes can splay. aim for ~ 1 cm ( thumb \u2019 s width ) of space in front of your longest toe. try shoes later in the day when feet are a bit swollen. - widths : if you have wide feet, look for models offered in wider fittings ( d, 4e ) or a foot - shaped toe box. - cushion & stack height : more foam = softer ride and more protection ; less = closer - to - ground feel. pick what \u2019 s comfortable for your weekly mileage and confidence level. - heel - to - toe drop : 0 \u2013 6 mm is low ( more natural stride ) ; 8 \u2013 12 + mm gives more heel cushioning. try both to see what feels natural. - stability & flexibility : choose stability features if you overpronate ; check forefoot flexibility for comfort in toe - off. - weight & responsiveness : lighter shoes feel quicker ; some foams balance softness and energy return. - durability & outsole :",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0000-34-18209:6": -1.1711534261703491,
            "clueweb22-en0001-46-09453:0": -1.1978759765625,
            "clueweb22-en0016-05-18811:0": -1.539739727973938,
            "clueweb22-en0040-12-07254:2": -1.6907596588134766,
            "clueweb22-en0044-30-08869:1": -1.9571495056152344
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s a lot of things to consider! Are there any brands you would avoid for beginners?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Short answer: avoid minimalist/zero\u2011drop and hardcore racing shoes as a beginner.  What to steer clear of - Zero\u2011drop or very minimalist brands/models (e.g., many Altra/Topo/Merrell minimalist lines) \u2014 they have little heel cushion and demand a developed mid/forefoot technique, which raises injury risk for new runners.   - Racing flats, spikes, or ultra\u2011light \u201cperformance\u201d trainers \u2014 great for experienced racers, but too little cushioning for routine beginner miles.   - Casual slip\u2011ons or fashion sneakers not built for running \u2014 they can look comfy but often lack shock absorption and stability for regular training.  What to choose instead - Cushioned, stable, and roomy shoes with a forgiving midsole (examples: mainstream New Balance, Brooks Ghost, or beginner models from Decathlon/Kalenji).   - Try shoes later in the day, get ~1 cm toe room, and test walk/run in them before buying.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0035-42-12749:2": 0.20652803778648376,
            "clueweb22-en0032-86-15272:2": -1.1718558073043823,
            "clueweb22-en0024-47-14566:12": -1.3170771598815918,
            "clueweb22-en0017-51-11925:9": -1.4672842025756836,
            "clueweb22-en0023-47-02332:6": -1.6601468324661255
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Cool, that's useful. By the way, what about warming up? Any simple routines to follow before running?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 aim for dynamic warm-ups (movement-based) rather than long static stretches. Quick options you can pick depending on time:  1) Quick (2\u20133 min) \u2014 for short easy runs - Brisk walk 2 min - Leg swings (forward/back and side-to-side) 5\u201310 each leg - Gentle hip circles 8 each side  2) Standard (5\u201310 min) \u2014 general pre-run - Brisk walk or easy jog 3\u20134 min - Walking lunges 8\u201310 steps each leg - Bodyweight squats 10 - Hamstring sweeps 8\u201310 each leg - Leg swings 8\u201310 each leg - Arm swings/shoulder windmills 30\u201360s  3) If doing speed/HIIT or longer runs, add agility drills (30\u201360s shuffles, high knees, 6\u201310 tuck jumps) and 1\u20132 short striders.  Tips: don\u2019t round your back on dead\u2011lift style moves; stop any exercise that hurts; try barefoot only if comfortable; save static holds for after your run. For long runs, spend closer to 10 minutes warming up. Start small and build the routine as your confidence grows.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-73-01073:10": -0.16358116269111633,
            "clueweb22-en0003-84-06062:1": -0.3117404580116272,
            "clueweb22-en0006-62-05770:1": -0.4752174913883209,
            "clueweb22-en0001-95-00540:3": -1.188583254814148,
            "clueweb22-en0008-15-10957:2": -1.3485065698623657
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s easy to understand. I tend to get a runny nose when I run, especially in summer. Any tips for that?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can I adjust my workout plan for a 5km run?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Sounds like exercise\u2011induced rhinitis made worse by seasonal pollen \u2014 common in summer. Practical options to try:  - Medication: an OTC antihistamine (or a daily one during high\u2011pollen weeks) often helps \u2014 check with your doctor/pharmacist first.   - Nasal care: saline rinse (neti pot or spray) before/after runs to wash out pollen and loosen mucus.   - Nasal sprays: some people use a nasal steroid or a pre\u2011run nasal spray (ask your clinician) to reduce symptoms.   - Timing & route: run later in the day (after 10 a.m.) or after rain when pollen is lower; swap outdoor runs for indoor workouts on high\u2011pollen days.   - Practical tweaks: wear sunglasses or a lightweight buff to shield your nose/eyes, carry tissues, and stay well hydrated.   - See a clinician if you get fever, wheeze, very itchy eyes, or persistent problems despite these measures.  If you want, I can suggest a simple pre\u2011run routine (including a saline rinse + 5\u2011minute warm up) to try for a week and we can track how it goes.",
          "citations": {
